<p>
<b>
Legal consultancies</b><p>
I have twice served as an &quot;expert witness&quot; in cases related
to trade names in Spanish and English. The cases involved lawsuits
(totaling more than 500 million dollars) against a large
multinational firm, which was accused of selling a dangerous product
in Latin America. Corpus-based data that I provided showed, however,
that the company's trade name had become generic (like xerox or
kleenex), so that people who used another company's product
mistakenly called it by the generic name of the product in question.
Partially as a result of the corpus data that I presented, the
lawsuits against this company were dismissed. (Note that because I have created the corpora that are used for
these and similar cases, I can retrieve data from the corpora in
ways that might not be possible for other linguist expert witnesses.)



<p>
<b>
Corpora and the law</b>
<p>
<a name="legal"></a>Linguistic data from
<a target="_top" href="http://www.english-corpora.org/">my corpora</a> have
been used in a number of court cases, including a case from the US
Supreme Court in February 2011 (see
<a target="_top" href="http://lawnlinguistics.wordpress.com/2011/01/19/personal-privacy-&#8800;-corporate-privacy/">
overview</a>,
<a target="_top" href="http://www.americanbar.org/content/dam/aba/publishing/preview/publiced_preview_briefs_pdfs_09_10_09_1279_PetitionerAmCuPOGO_BrechnerCtr_andTaxAnalystsnew.authcheckdam.pdf">
amicus brief</a>,
<a target="_top" href="http://www.theatlantic.com/national/archive/2011/03/the-corpus-in-the-court-like-lexis-on-steroids/72054/">
Atlantic</a>,
<a target="_top" href="http://languagelog.ldc.upenn.edu/nll/?p=3002">
Language Log</a>). They have also been used as the basis for some
articles in law reviews, such as
<a target="_top" href="http://digitalcommons.law.byu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=2561&context=lawreview">this</a> and
<a target="_blank" href="http://stlr.org/download/volumes/volume13/Mouritsen.pdf">
this</a>. Stephen Mouritsen (a former student of mine) and Tom Lee
(of the Utah Supreme Court, whom I have worked with) have a series of articles in the
<a href="https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/volokh-conspiracy/wp/2017/08/07/justice-thomas-rex-lee-and-stephen-mouritsen-guest-blogging-about-corpus-linguistics-and-the-law/">
Washington Post</a> about corpora and legal studies (this is in
addition to many other newspaper articles, such as
<a href="https://www.deseret.com/article/700118257/BYU-professor-Mark-Davies-has-new-way-of-seeing-words.html">
this one</a>). Finally, the BYU Law School has started a series of
<a href="http://lawwww.english-corpora.org/">conferences based on legal
studies using corpora</a>, and much of this is based on the BYU
corpora. In a very real sense, BYU and the BYU Law School is at the
forefront of using corpora to study the law.




<p><b>
Technology</b><p>In order to create
large corpora and place them online, I have acquired experience in a
number of different technologies.&nbsp; These include database
organization and optimization (mainly with
<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_SQL_Server">SQL Server</a>,
including advanced <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SQL">SQL
queries</a>), web-database integration (<a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ActiveX_Data_Objects">ActiveX
Data Objects</a>), client-side programming (mainly
<a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_HTML">DHTML</a> /
<a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JavaScript">Javascript</a>),
<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visual_Basic_.NET">VB.NET</a>
(for processing billions of words of data) and several different corpus and text-related tools
(like <a href="http://www.laurenceanthony.net/software/antconc/">
AntConc</a>
